<SiteLayout
  title="Ejido X-Hazil cooperative story"
  description="A source-grounded account of Ejido X-Hazil y Anexos, its forestry history, chicle practices, and documented community institutions."
>
  <PageHeader
    title="Ejido X-Hazil: a documented forest history."
    marker="Cooperative archive · ES source"
    introduction="This account summarizes the supplied Spanish history by Tropica Rural Latinoamericana, A.C. It does not turn historical forestry evidence into claims about a current retreat or tourism cooperative."
  />

  <article class="content-section reading-column">
    <h2>A territory beside Sian Ka'an.</h2>
    <p>
      The source records Ejido X-Hazil y Anexos as founded in 1936 by Maya
      communities following the Caste War. It names three settlements—Chancah
      Veracruz, X-Hazil, and Uh May—and records 398 ejidatarios across a
      55,020-hectare territory bordering the Sian Ka'an Biosphere Reserve.
    </p>

    <blockquote lang="es">
      <p>
        “El ejido X-Hazil y Anexos fue fundado en 1936… Actualmente cuenta con
        398 ejidatarios distribuidos en tres centros de población.”
      </p>
      <footer>
        Preserved Spanish excerpt · Historia del Ejido X-Hazil Sur
      </footer>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>A working timeline.</h2>
    <ol class="timeline">
      <li><time datetime="1936">1936</time><span>Founding recorded in the supplied history.</span></li>
      <li><time datetime="1946">1946</time><span>Six beekeepers organized a 20-hive apiary with a 2,000-peso cooperative credit.</span></li>
      <li><time datetime="1985">1985</time><span>The Plan Piloto Forestal enters the documented governance timeline.</span></li>
      <li><time datetime="1986">1986</time><span>X-Hazil becomes a founding member of OEPFZM, the regional forest-producer organization named in the source.</span></li>
      <li><time datetime="1990">1990</time><span>The ejido acquires its own sawmill.</span></li>
      <li><time datetime="2003">2003</time><span>A 1,434-hectare ejidal forest reserve is recorded adjoining Sian Ka'an.</span></li>
      <li><time datetime="2005">2005</time><span>Good Forest Management certificate SW-FM/COC-1517 is recorded as obtained in May.</span></li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Forestry, milpa, chicle, and beekeeping.</h2>
    <p>
      The document describes livelihoods shaped by milpa agriculture, chicle
      extraction, beekeeping, and forest management. It records a permanent
      forestry area of 25,000 hectares and a historical chicle cooperative.
      Forestry is described as supporting around 100 seasonal jobs at the time
      of the source.
    </p>

    <SourceNote label="Time-sensitive source">
      <p>
        The document states that the last referenced chicle-harvest permit had
        expired and required a new technical study. Current permit, employment,
        forestry, and certification status must be re-verified before launch
        copy presents them as active.
      </p>
    </SourceNote>

    <h2>What this history does not prove.</h2>
    <p>
      The source does not document an operating eco-touristic retreat, a
      tourism cooperative, the Mayan Learning Center, the Mayan Bee Sanctuary,
      present-day bookings, or current biodiversity credits. The retreat
      record on this site therefore remains a concept under documentation.
    </p>

